xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * NOT RELEASED.  This stanza describes the tree since the v0.2 tag.  The
    version and the date stamp get settled when 0.3 is cut; until then a build
    of this tree produces a 0.3 package that has not been published anywhere.

  * xigmaui in three columns instead of one long scroll, at the author's own
    1 919x1 055: the clip list had a 190 px viewport with 114 px rows, which is
    two clips on screen, and `ResizeMode.Adjust` painted the selected row
    as a block taller than the list itself.  Rows are 66 px now (64 px
    thumbnails, `Fixed`, uniform, and every row *told* its height - uniform
    sizing latches the first row's hint before any thumbnail exists).  The clip
    library is its own column, the log sits under the preview where the height
    cannot be used for anything else, and a fresh window opens at 1180x780.
    Measured rows in view: 7 at the default, 12 at 1 919x1 055, where it was 2.

  * `advanced controls`: one checkbox, default off, folding six secondary things
    - canvas, scrim, fan figures, the display's event ring, the panel's `conn 1`
    hello, the 30 s frame run.  Pixels only, never values: Apply sends what is
    hidden.  The guarded factory reset is deliberately never folded away.

  * Thumbnails are letterboxed rather than stretched: 3 of the 43 clips the
    themes package installs are 320x240, and drawing a 4:3 frame as a square
    contradicted the `320x240` printed on the same row.

  * The text this program draws can be another colour, from all three doors:
    `--text-color` on the display's command line, `{"cmd": "text_color",
    "value": "#ff4040"}` on the control socket, and a `text colour` row in the
    window's theme box - its own palette by name, with swatches, or the colour
    dialog.  One parser, `render.parse_ink`, so the doors cannot drift.  Our own
    layer only: a vendor theme's lettering is decided inside that theme's layout
    file, and those are not all white either - 88 of the 106 text colours in the
    library's 23 480x480 layouts are white, and the suite re-derives that count
    now the claim is in the documents.  Measured off glass: an inked frame's
    painted area is unchanged, every glyph core moves to the chosen colour, and
    the ink survives the default 256-colour palette encoding within 1/255 per
    channel.  Measured on glass too: five inks over the control socket at 1 fps,
    80 frames, 0 refused, `space` unchanged the whole way, and the colours read
    as the colours sent - `data/glass/2026-11-07-102041-ink-on-glass-watch.txt`.

  * Against a display too old to know the new command - the installed 0.2
    service driven by this window, which is the author's own machine - the row
    goes inactive with the reason, Apply sends what that display does know and
    says which one it left alone, and the state line reads `text not reported by
    this display` rather than the misleading `text theme default`.

  * The preview pane plays.  A `playing` box above the picture decodes the
    background in sequence through one streaming ffmpeg (`clips.ClipPlayer`) and
    composites our layer over each frame; still mode keeps the seek-to-mid-loop
    thumbnail path, which is the right shape for one image and an order of
    magnitude worse for a loop - twelve frames out of one process took 0.03 s,
    while twelve seeks cost the better part of a second, and the seek is what
    would hitch.  The rate comes from the file: `_ffprobe_look` now asks ffprobe
    for `r_frame_rate` too, and of the 43 clips the themes package installs 23
    are 24 fps and 20 are 20 fps (17 of the 480x480 ones plus all three
    320x240), so a hard-coded 24 would play nearly half the library at the wrong
    speed.  Two clocks, measured rather than assumed: the layer redraws at
    `--preview-fps` (85.6 ms a tick, 71.8 ms of it `sensors.collect`) and the
    background plays on its own `QTimer` at the file's rate (41 ms for a 24 fps
    clip), pasting the cached layer over each decoded frame - a background tick
    is 0.54 ms (decode 1.06 ms with it, composite 0.26 ms, an identity
    480x480 resize 0.02 ms).  The first cut drove both from the preview timer,
    which made the animation's rate equal `--preview-fps`: on this bench's own
    saved `preview_fps: 1.0` that was a 1 fps slideshow, correctly reported as
    "very slow and stutters at about 1 fps", and the split takes the same window
    to 24.3 fps of background with a 1 fps layer (23.3 at 2).  Rate capped at
    30, floored at 5; a file whose rate ffprobe will not give is played at 24
    and the line under the picture says so.  A hidden window decodes nothing:
    `hideEvent` closes the ffmpeg and stops both timers, 2 s hidden shows and
    decodes zero frames, and a `black` canvas never opens a stream because no
    background shows there.  Remembered as `animate`.  The seam the viewer sees
    is the file's own and is not a splice: 23-24 frames a pass, and the
    first frame of three successive passes is byte-identical
    (f7e5ded0b703).  The two lines under the picture change with the
    state rather than at the next preview tick: a window at `preview_fps
    1.0` otherwise went on saying "one mid-loop frame" for a second
    after `playing` was ticked or the canvas switched, and the caption's
    second half had no separator before the file's own note, printing
    "...measures those.background Theme_25.mp4 at mid-loop".  Both were
    caught by the suite's new assertions - one of them as a race, which
    is the honest way to find it - and the caption now names the loop's
    own facts instead: `Theme_25.mp4 is 0.96 s at 24 fps, 480x480`.
    Measured with the layer clock stopped and the background's running:
    46 frames reached the label in 2.0 s at a 1 fps layer.

  * tools/preview_gif.py writes that composite out as an animated GIF, and the
    README's five theme previews are GIFs now - 320 px, 12 frames at a 170 ms
    delay, 128 colours, 471 305-585 559 B each, 2.04 s - where the five still
    PNGs used to be; the PNGs are still written by tools/previews.py and are no
    longer tracked.  GIF because GitHub renders an MP4 written as a markdown
    image link as an `<img>` no browser plays.  320 px because GitHub displays
    this README's body 624 px wide in a 1 000 px window and showed the old
    480 px PNGs at 281-289 px, so 480 px GIFs (1.05-1.18 MB each) buy nothing at
    the size they are drawn.  128 colours because it measures 23 % smaller than
    256 with the drawn colour within 1/255 at 480 px either way.  And the loop
    join is measured with PROTOCOL.md's own seam metric rather than trusted:
    2.05-2.36 against typical steps of 2.15-2.52 on the six shipped files,
    against 2.50/1.51 for the same clip with nothing drawn on it.  docs/gui.md
    carries an ink figure - alpha over the vendored clip, text in #ffc400 -
    whose nearest drawn colour measures 2/255 from the amber asked for; that is
    ffmpeg's animation palette and not the 256-colour PNG the upload path uses,
    and the documents say so beside the picture rather than leaving it assumed.

  * tools/selftest.sh, nineteen offline sections; tools/screenshot.py frames the
    window at 1380x860 and finishes probing the rows a picture can show;
    tools/display_glass.py's Apply phase sends the colour command along with the
    rest, so the next bench run can settle the glass half.  The new section
    checks the README's GIFs as documents (320x320, 12 frames, 170 ms, under
    700 KB, a join no larger than an ordinary frame step, ink within 3/255 of
    what was asked) and pointedly not by byte hash, runs the tool for all five
    themes at 160 px - the themes take different render options, and passing one
    option bag straight through died with `theme_alpha() got an unexpected
    keyword argument 'source'` - and asserts the two clocks are separate: with
    the layer timer stopped, a window at `preview_fps 1.0` has to move the
    picture at least ten times in two seconds while its layer tick count does
    not move at all.

  * The clip list is one row per distinct clip, not one per path.  With the
    themes package installed and the Windows partition mounted, the roots the
    window walks held 161 mp4 files for 40 clips, so it showed 161 rows for 40
    backgrounds.  Three kinds of copy: the Windows app re-copies a library clip
    into its `media/background/` under a millisecond name every time a theme is
    applied (all 26 `175749*.mp4` there are byte-identical to a `Theme_NN` or
    `theme_NN` clip, whole-file sha256); the vendor library repeats inside the
    copy this repository carries (`Theme_31.mp4` == `Theme_26.mp4`,
    `theme_38.mp4` == `Theme_27.mp4`, `theme_51.mp4` == `Theme_29.mp4`); and the
    installed packages sit next to the checkout holding the same bytes again.
    Those 26 are deliberately NOT deleted from the Windows volume: the vendor
    app's own `store.json` names 31 of them as a theme background, and a program
    that deleted them would leave that app holding paths to files that are not
    there.  So the collapse happens in this program's list, keyed on length then
    sha256 of the first and last MiB (whole file under 2 MiB) - the older
    (name, length) rule could not see a renamed copy, and left 69 rows standing.
    Within one root the better-named file wins, because timestamp names sort
    before `Theme_*` (digits before letters); across roots the precedence order
    stands, so the row shown is the file `find_media()` would have chosen.
    Measured: 161 files -> 40 rows, 121 copies dropped, 35 of them under a
    different name - and 40 is exactly what an independent audit of all 161
    files by whole-file sha256 found distinct.  The window says the numbers out
    loud in its log, because a list that quietly gets shorter looks like a bug.
 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 09:45:00 +0200
xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.2) resolute; urgency=medium

  * README.md is the overview and the detail lives in docs/ - install.md,
    usage.md, gui.md, themes.md, packaging.md, tools.md, limits.md,
    troubleshooting.md - cut out of the old README by line range rather than
    rewritten, so no measured figure changed on the way.  155 lines of README
    and a nine-row table of where the rest is.

  * The links are checked, because a split is exactly the kind of change that
    breaks a relative path while leaving valid markdown: tools/selftest.sh
    resolves every link in README.md and every docs/*.md (44 of them across ten
    documents), and debian/tests/smoke does the same for the installed layout.

  * docs/screenshot-xigmaui.png, made by tools/screenshot.py: the real window,
    built offscreen the way the suite builds it, with a device path that must
    not exist so that taking the picture never opens anyone's port.

  * The package installs the documents: debian/rules stages them as
    /usr/share/doc/xigmatek-connect-lcd/docs/ because debian/<package>.docs
    installs by basename, and override_dh_compress leaves the markdown and the
    transcripts uncompressed (dh_compress -X.md -X.log) because gzipped ones
    break every relative link in the set.  The images stay out on purpose.

  * Reproducible archives, measured rather than asserted.  debian/changelog's
    0.1 entry was dated ahead of the build that built it, and dpkg-deb pulls
    member mtimes down to SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH while leaving earlier ones alone -
    which is how the published v0.1 asset and a build of the same tree came out
    18 B apart with every file md5 matching.  The 0.1 entry now carries the time
    it was written; the staged trees are touched to the changelog's stamp before
    dh_builddeb; the build job fails if the two packages do not carry exactly
    one member date, equal to that stamp; and a build of 8ac3a32 here and the
    runner's build of 8ac3a32 are the same bytes (5 165 848 B, sha256
    bf887c0eb296c481730a125c8234c6e8584c8f5fb9b69d4f4abb2c46d33fe264).

  * tools/selftest.sh grew with it: the glass-ledger checks read the docs that
    quote the runs, the changelog stamp must not be in the future, the package's
    own __version__ has to agree with pyproject.toml, the PyQt6-absent path is
    checked by hiding PyQt6 behind an import blocker, and the clip-root check
    asserts the rule instead of the state of one machine.  Seventeen offline
    sections, and README, docs/packaging.md, debian/changelog and CI all agree
    on that number.

 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 06:54:31 +0200

xigmatek-connect-lcd (0.1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * First packaged form of the project, and the first form that is not a git
    checkout: the display loop (`xigmalcd`), the Qt window (`xigmaui`), the
    three background clips this project vendors, the udev rule, the systemd
    user unit and the menu entry all land in the normal places.
  * The vendor's panel reset ships as its own command, `xigmalcd-reset`,
    because an installed system has no other way back once the panel's
    `logo`/`mode` read 2147483647 - those fields cannot be set over the wire,
    only reset.  It sends nothing without --yes, the shipped unit does not
    arm --allow-reset, and debian/tests/smoke fails if either ever changes.
    tools/reset_panel.py is now the checkout spelling of that same code
    (`xigmalcd/reset.py`) rather than a second copy of it.
  * Data discovery had to become installation-aware first: a path next to the
    checkout is not a thing after `apt install`, so the clip roots are now
    `$XIGMA_MEDIA`, the checkout's `media/` and /usr/share/xigmalcd/media,
    first that exists, with the Windows partition's copies behind them
    (`xigmalcd/main.py:media_roots`).  The window finds its icon the same way:
    the checkout file, then the hicolor copy this package installs.
    tools/selftest.sh checks those rules against fake roots.
  * `xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes` carries ALPHA's own theme library - 27
    `.thm` files as shipped, their decrypted layouts, 44 background clips, 27
    baked overlay PNGs, and the manifest that hashes all 152 entries of the
    125 distinct files they name - copied byte for byte out of the vendor
    installation on the author's bench.  Those bytes are XIGMATEK's, not
    ours, and `debian/copyright` says so instead of pretending to license
    them; the package exists so a user can take them down again in one
    command.
  * Everything measured about the protocol is in `PROTOCOL.md`, and the
    offline suite (`tools/selftest.sh`, seventeen offline sections) is what
    CI runs before it builds a package.

 -- Tamer Hassan <thameruddin@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 05:10:42 +0200
